 "issue": 472,
 "issue_title": "Assembly lenses reappear as standalone library lenses; every full save logs ERROR per assembly lens",
 "issue_body": "**Where:** `src/database.py:539-557`, `:759-762`; `src/gui/storage.py:181-191`\n\n`load_all` returns every row in `lenses` as a top-level `\"Lens\"`, including lenses that only exist inside an assembly. They show up in GUI and CLI lens lists after reload. On a full save, `_reconcile` tries to delete each one and gets `LensInUseError`; `delete_item` logs any exception at ERROR before re-raising, so each save produces an ERROR line per assembly lens.\n\n**Repro:** after saving `[OpticalSystem A(L1, L2)]` and reloading: `[('Lens','L1'), ('Lens','L2'), ('OpticalSystem','A')]`.\n\n**Fix:** decide whether a lens belongs to the library or only to an assembly (e.g. a `standalone` column, or exclude lenses referenced only by assemblies). At minimum, don't log `LensInUseError` at ERROR.",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/pmaasz/openLens/issues/472",
 "pr": 532,
 "pr_title": "fix(db): an assembly's lenses must not reappear in the library",
 "pr_body": "## Description\n\nfix(db): an assembly's lenses must not reappear in the library\n\n`load_all` returned every row in `lenses` as a top-level `\"Lens\"`, including lenses that exist only as assembly elements. Saving `[OpticalSystem A(L1, L2)]` and reloading produced:\n\n```\n[('Lens','L1'), ('Lens','L2'), ('OpticalSystem','A')]\n```\n\nfor a library that held only `A`.\n\n### The fix is not a filter on \"referenced by an assembly\"\n\nThat hides a lens the user deliberately kept in the library **and** put in an assembly \u2014 my first attempt, and a test caught it. The rows genuinely cannot say which is which: both are lens rows. So `lenses.is_library` (schema v6) records what only the caller knows \u2014 and `save_lenses` is handed the library snapshot on every save.\n\nAssignment is **opt-in** via `library_snapshot`, for exactly the reason `reconcile` already is. This is not hypothetical: **four existing tests broke** on my first version, which assigned unconditionally. The CLI passes a lens-only list, so reading \"absent from this list\" as \"not a library item\" there would erase the entire library.\n\nMigration defaults existing rows to `1`, so old databases behave exactly as before until the application next saves.\n\n### Also in this change\n\n- **`delete_item` logged `LensInUseError` at ERROR.** That is the guard working as designed, so every full save of a library with assemblies reported one failure per assembly lens while behaving perfectly correctly. Now DEBUG; genuine failures stay ERROR, and a test drives the real error path to prove it rather than asserting something that cannot fail.\n- **`_reconcile` no longer attempts deletes the in-use guard will refuse.** A doomed attempt is not a safety net \u2014 it is one refusal per assembly lens per save.\n- **Seven tests hard-coded `user_version=5`.** They now assert `SCHEMA_VERSION`, so the next schema bump is not a chase through five files by hand.\n\n## Type of Change\n\n- [x] Bug fix (non-breaking change which fixes an issue)\n\n## Related Issues\n\nCloses #472\n\n## How Has This Been Tested?\n\n- [x] Added a regression test that fails before this change and passes after\n- [x] Verified by reverting **each part separately**:\n\n  | Reverted | Failures (of 19) |\n  | --- | --- |\n  | `load_all` ignores `is_library` (the reported bug) | 3 |\n  | membership never recorded | 3 |\n  | `LensInUseError` back at ERROR | 1 |\n  | reconcile attempts doomed deletes | 1 |\n\n- [x] `python3 -m unittest tests.test_assembly_lens_library_membership` \u2014 19 tests, OK\n- [x] `test_delete_row_removal` + `test_optimization_apply` + `test_database` +\n      `test_database_migrations` \u2014 72 tests, OK (all four of the regressions above)\n- [x] Full suite: `Ran 2047 tests`, `FAILED (errors=6, skipped=34)` \u2014 the same six\n      pre-existing `tests/gui_flows/test_*` loader errors, no new failures\n- [x] `black --line-length=100 src/ tests/ --check` \u2014 245 files unchanged\n- [x] `flake8` \u2014 no new findings beyond one `E402` from the added import, matching\n      that file's existing layout\n- [x] Coverage includes: the issue's repro, no phantom names, a genuinely standalone\n      lens still loading, **a lens shared between the library and an assembly still\n      loading**, two assemblies sharing a lens, the assembly keeping its elements,\n      every element resolving to its own lens data, a save/reload round trip being\n      stable, the refusal still raising, **a refusal not logged at ERROR**, a real\n      failure still logged at ERROR, the refusal explained at DEBUG, the helper\n      reporting assembly lenses, reconcile attempting no deletes, reconcile still\n      removing genuinely stale rows, the assembly surviving reconciliation, and\n      `all_ids` still seeing everything\n\n### Two tests I had to rewrite because they were not testing what they claimed\n\n`test_a_genuine_delete_failure_is_still_logged_at_error` asserted\n`isinstance(errors, list)` \u2014 it could not fail. It now patches the connection so\n`delete_item` genuinely raises a non-`LensInUseError`, and asserts the ERROR line\nappears.\n\n`test_the_refusal_is_explained_at_debug` used `min(logger.level, level)`. With\n`NOTSET` (0) that computes 0, leaving the level at `NOTSET` and deferring to the\nroot logger \u2014 WARNING. So the debug records it existed to check were silently\ndiscarded, and the test failed for a reason unrelated to what it was about.\n\n### One existing test was querying through the wrong view\n\n`tests/test_optimization_apply._rows_for` used `LensStorage.load_lenses()` to\ncheck that a row persisted. That is the *library* view, and an assembly element\nis deliberately not a library item now \u2014 so it reported \"absent\" for a row stored\nperfectly well. It reads the table directly, which is what a persistence assertion\nshould do.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] My code follows the style guidelines\n- [x] I have performed a self-review\n- [x] I have commented my code, particularly in hard-to-understand areas\n- [ ] I have made corresponding changes to the documentation\n- [ ] My changes generate no new warnings\n- [x] I have added tests that prove my fix is effective\n- [x] New and existing unit tests pass locally with my changes",
